  "observations": {
    "whatHappened": "45° gave the longest range. 30° and 60° landed in the same place. With air resistance the path was lopsided and shorter. On the Moon it flew about six times further.",
    "why": "Without air the horizontal speed stays constant while gravity changes the vertical speed. The range is v² sin 2θ / g, which is largest at 45° and the same for θ and 90° − θ. Drag slows the ball throughout, so it falls short and comes down more steeply.",
    "equationLatex": "R = \\frac{v^2 \\sin 2\\theta}{g} \\qquad H = \\frac{v^2 \\sin^2 \\theta}{2g}",
    "realWorld": [
      "Long jump and javelin angles",
      "Basketball shots",
      "Fountains and water jets"
    ],
    "prompts": [
